# Agent 2 Scientific Safety Rules

## Never invent missing biology

If the Agent 1 handoff does not carry a piece of information (a kinetic
value, a regulatory interaction, a cofactor classification, a MetaCyc/
BioCyc-sourced identifier), never fabricate a plausible replacement.
Represent it as absent (`None`, an empty tuple, or a `PLACEHOLDER`/
`DEFAULT` `ParameterSpecification`).

## Preserve provenance

Every element traceable to Agent 1 (a reaction, a species, a parameter's
curated value) must carry forward its provenance reference. Never strip a
provenance link while reshaping data into an Agent 2 contract type.

## Mark assumptions explicitly

Any assumption Agent 2 makes to produce a `ModuleSpecification`,
`ModuleBoundaryInterface`, or Antimony artifact must be recorded in that
object's own `assumptions`/`assumption` field — never left implicit in
code comments only.

## Distinguish parameter provenance/status

Always use the correct `ParameterSource` for a parameter's origin:
`CURATED` (from Agent 1's curated data), `LITERATURE_DERIVED`, `DEFAULT`,
`PLACEHOLDER`, or `CALIBRATED` (only for a value returned by Agent 4's
feedback). Never present a `PLACEHOLDER`/`DEFAULT` value as though it were
`CURATED`/`CALIBRATED`.

## Never treat boundary likelihood as probability

`BoundaryLikelihood` values are qualitative heuristic judgments produced
by explicit, versioned rules. Never convert one to a number, average
several together, or otherwise treat the vocabulary as a probability
distribution.

## Never silently invent boundary conditions

A standalone module Antimony artifact requires fully explicit
`ModuleBoundaryInterface` declarations. If they are not present, do not
guess a boundary condition to make the module simulatable — omit the
standalone artifact for that module instead (module views remain
available regardless).
